# Files whose bytes are hash-pinned (release/organ-proof-manifest.json,
# tests/test_organ_release_conformance.py) must check out identically on
# every platform. Without this, Windows' core.autocrlf=true converts LF to
# CRLF on checkout, producing a different sha256 than Linux/macOS -- which
# only stores/checks out the committed LF bytes -- even though the git
# object itself is unchanged.
.aios/state/ORGAN_GREEN_LEDGER.json text eol=lf
# condition_proof_budget.json is read by a gate and diffed on every
# migration step, so it gets the same LF pin rather than picking up
# CRLF from whichever platform last lowered it.
.aios/state/*.json text eol=lf
.aios/state/*.md text eol=lf
docs/architecture/GAGOS_54_ORGANS.md text eol=lf
release/**/*.json text eol=lf
# The phase5 proof docs are generated, not hand-written, so without this a
# Windows run of verify_organ_twelve_conditions.py restates all 54 of them in
# CRLF and the real change disappears into the noise.
release/**/*.md text eol=lf

# Shell scripts must check out LF on every platform. `scripts/install.sh` is
# committed with LF and Linux clones get LF, but core.autocrlf=true gives a
# Windows contributor CRLF in the working tree -- and a CRLF shebang is
# `/usr/bin/env: 'bash\r': No such file or directory` on the box the installer
# exists to serve. Measured 2026-09-07 by running the working-tree copy in a
# clean Fedora container.
*.sh text eol=lf
# The security spine hashes its own BYTES. `boot_attestation.compute_spine_hash`
# reads every .py in aios/security/ with read_bytes(), so the same commit hashes
# differently depending on how it was checked out -- measured 2026-09-07:
#
#   pure-LF   checkout   c980c996954a5d2b959e1110bf1f6c2e9418594e00ac0f5be2a3188adf0e9666
#   pure-CRLF checkout   c5ff39132be09df80433b53f5bc512d24493d685297de29ef1e9fc56ab22336f
#
# That makes the spine hash a fact about a working tree rather than about the
# code, so two honest machines report different values for identical bytes and
# the number cannot be compared or published.
#
# Pinning here fixes it WITHOUT touching aios/security/ or
# boot_attestation.py, both of which are frozen-core (AGENTS.md SS VIII) and
# operator-attested. Every checkout now yields the LF value.
#
# ONE-TIME COST, stated rather than hidden: a tree that currently holds CRLF
# flips to LF, so the next `attest_boot` compares against a log entry made
# under the old bytes and reports TAMPERED once. That is the fail-closed
# control working correctly on a real byte change. The attestation log is NOT
# edited to suppress it -- resisting exactly that edit is what the log is for.
aios/security/*.py text eol=lf
